  faqHeading: "Hiring a web developer in Dubai: what buyers ask before they commit",
  faqs: [
    { question: "What does it cost to hire a web developer in Dubai?", answer: `It depends which of four routes you mean. Employing someone costs a salary every month plus visa, insurance, gratuity accrual, equipment and management time. An agency costs a build plus a retainer. A resident freelancer costs a build and carries availability risk. With BtechWaleTech a static site starts from ${P.site} with upkeep from ${P.care} and nothing recurring.` },
    { question: "Is it cheaper to employ a developer or outsource the work in Dubai?", answer: "Employment is cheaper when the work is continuous, because the salary is spread over constant output. Outsourcing is cheaper when the work is project-shaped, because you pay for output rather than for availability. The test is simple: count how many weeks in the next year genuinely need a developer. Below roughly half, a salary is paying for idle capacity you cannot recover." },
    { question: "What hidden costs sit behind a Dubai developer's salary?", answer: "End-of-service gratuity accruing from month thirteen under Federal Decree-Law No. 33 of 2021, thirty days of annual leave, sick leave, employer-provided health insurance, the work permit and residence visa cycle, payroll through the Wage Protection System, recruitment, a laptop and software, and the management time somebody spends directing the work each week." },
    { question: "How much is the end-of-service gratuity for an employee in the UAE?", answer: "Under Federal Decree-Law No. 33 of 2021, an employee completing a year of continuous service is entitled to twenty-one calendar days of basic salary for each of the first five years and thirty calendar days for each year after, with the total capped at two years of pay. It accrues from month thirteen, so provision for it monthly rather than treating it as a leaving cost." },
    { question: "How do I verify a web developer in Dubai before paying anything?", answer: "Seven paper checks. Ask for the trade licence or freelance permit and read the activity and expiry. Match the bank account name to the licence and the quote. Verify the tax registration number if VAT is charged. Look up their own domain's registration record. Open their own site on your phone and use its contact form. Check how long the practice has existed. Open two live URLs and call one reference you chose." },
    { question: "How should payments to a web developer be staged?", answer: "So that at every stage enough of the fee is still unpaid to make finishing worth more than leaving. Amounts should fall rather than rise: a small first payment that buys a written specification you can keep, a moderate one when the design direction is approved on a real page in a browser, a moderate one when the build is complete on a staging link you have tested, and the balance after handover." },
    { question: "What is a holdback and should I ask for one?", answer: "A modest percentage of the total held for a defined window after launch and released when the defect list clears. It is ordinary in construction procurement across this market and there is no reason web development should be the exception. Write down how long it is held, what releases it, and that it releases automatically if nothing is reported, because a holdback with no trigger is just an unpaid invoice." },
    { question: "How do I tell whether I actually own my existing website?", answer: "Six checks. Read the registrant organisation on your domain's registration record, then confirm you hold the registrar login, which is a separate question. Check whether you control the account holding your name servers. Check you are an administrator and not a viewer on your analytics property. Check your company is a verified owner in the search console. Check whose email opened the hosting and gateway accounts." },
    { question: "My former developer holds my domain. How do I get it back?", answer: "Request the authorisation code from the current registrar, unlock the domain and transfer it into a registrar account in your company's name. Expect procedural friction, including a transfer lock in the sixty days after a registration or a previous transfer. If the holder refuses outright, escalation runs through the registrar's dispute process and the registry's dispute policy for that namespace, and a UAE lawyer should drive it rather than a developer." },
    { question: "What are the signs a web developer is about to disappear?", answer: "In order: reply times stretching with plausible reasons, a staging link unchanged for a week while updates stay optimistic, progress described as activity rather than shown as output, conversation migrating to voice notes, a payment requested ahead of its milestone, an unannounced new name on the work, then silence. The accumulation is the signal, not any single item." },
    { question: "If something goes wrong, what recourse do I have against an offshore team?", answer: "Less than against a local supplier, and it is better to hear that before you pay than after. A licensed UAE entity can be escalated to its licensing authority and a permit holder to the authority that issued the permit. An overseas supplier realistically cannot be pursued economically for a project of this size. That asymmetry is exactly why the payment ladder and the holdback matter more on this route." },
    { question: "Do you hold a UAE trade licence, office or phone number?", answer: "No. BtechWaleTech is three freelance engineers working remotely from India, with no UAE office, no UAE trade licence, no UAE phone number and no staff in the country. We cannot attend your premises, photograph your products, collect a document or appear in a tender meeting. India is 1.5 hours ahead of Dubai, so calls and replies happen inside your working day." },
  ],
